libxtc (1.13.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Proc-teardown refcount: closes a use-after-free RACE CLASS at its
    shared root.  __resolve() handed out a struct xtc_proc * after
    releasing the table lock, so a concurrent exit could free it
    mid-delivery (a DOWN send, a cross-thread wake, a mailbox-stats
    read).  An atomic refcount taken while the table lock is held pins
    the proc across every resolve->deliver; the struct is freed only
    when the last reference drops.  Validated by the CI ASan job, which
    now runs with detect_stack_use_after_return=1 (previously blocked by
    this race).  New DST test test_sim_proc_teardown models the race
    deterministically.
  * Sanitizer fiber-switch annotations: both coro substrates (fcontext
    and ucontext) now call __sanitizer_start/finish_switch_fiber around
    every stack switch, so ASan/TSan/LSan track libxtc's user-space
    fiber switches instead of mis-attributing stack memory.  The full
    fiber runtime passes ASan with detect_stack_use_after_return=1 on
    both substrates -- previously impossible.  Zero cost / zero symbols
    in a non-sanitized build.  Requested by the threaded-PostgreSQL
    integration.
  * Windows cross-process spawn/monitor (xtc_xproc): a real port, no
    longer an XTC_E_NOSYS decline.  A portable child-entry registry
    (xtc_xproc_register_entry + xtc_xspawn_entry) addresses the child by
    a registered NAME the identical binary resolves in parent and child,
    bridging the absence of fork(); the Windows path re-execs via
    CreateProcess, uses a hardened loopback-TCP control channel (Winsock
    has no socketpair) with a per-spawn nonce, monitors exit via
    RegisterWaitForSingleObject, and maps the unhandled-exception
    NTSTATUS to a POSIX signal so a Windows crash surfaces as the same
    SIGNAL-kind DOWN as POSIX.  xtc_xproc_win_child_maybe() is wired into
    the embedder's main().
  * xtc_xlink: bidirectional cross-fork fate (xtc_link across the fork
    boundary), rounding out the xtc_xproc relation set.
  * xtc_tail runtime microscope: (a) the binary dump format is now
    compact AND portable -- a little-endian header + LEB128 delta-encoded
    events (~9 bytes/event vs a 32-byte struct), byte-identical across
    endianness and padding, matching dial9's space discipline; (b) the
    MSG source records send / receive / mailbox-depth high-water
    alongside the existing SCHED source; (c) a new offline viewer,
    tools/xtc-tail.py, reads / filters / steps / summarizes a captured
    trace (including --wake-latency to find lost/late wakeups), and the
    gdb xtc-tail-dump command writes a live program's ring for it.
  * Docs: a consolidated, honest Windows-vs-POSIX model-gap section
    (no fork, no socketpair, the int-fd Unix-ism, the DLL-export gap,
    unlink-of-open-file) in the Windows matrix.
  * New public API: xtc_xproc_register_entry, xtc_xspawn_entry,
    xtc_xlink, xtc_xproc_win_child_maybe; xtc_tail SEND/RECV/MBOX_HWM
    event kinds; XTC_DOWN_KIND_NOCONNECTION (from 1.12.0).  Sim suite
    grows to 51 deterministic tests.

 -- Greg Burd <greg@burd.me>  Sat, 11 Jul 2026 16:27:48 -0400

libxtc (1.12.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Crash-aware registry (the R5 remainder).  xtc_reg_reaper is a proc
    the embedder spawns once with the registry as its argument; it
    monitors every pid registered via xtc_reg_register_mon and
    auto-drops it (xtc_reg_drop_pid) when that pid goes DOWN -- the
    automatic form of the manual cleanup.  xtc_svr_call_name adds
    via-dispatch: address a gen_server by registered name instead of by
    pid (the Erlang {via, ...} / global-name pattern).  See xtc_reg(3).
  * Cross-fork spawn / send / monitor: xtc_xproc.  Extends the
    Erlang-style process relations across a fork() boundary -- a parent
    xtc_xspawn()s a child running its own xtc runtime, then xtc_xsend()s
    to it and xtc_xmonitor()s it as if it were a local process.  A child
    crash or exit surfaces as a normal, fully-classified xtc DOWN (exit
    code / signal decoded from the child's waitpid status), or
    XTC_DOWN_KIND_NOCONNECTION if the control channel dies first.  The
    single-host subset of the distributed design, over an xtc_osproc
    control socketpair rather than TCP.  POSIX only (Windows declines
    with XTC_E_NOSYS).  See xtc_xproc(3).
  * xtc_tail runtime microscope (phase 1).  A dial9-inspired recorder of
    individual runtime events tied to a precise instant -- the
    event-level complement to xtc_stats (aggregates) and xtc_trace
    (causal message trace).  Phase 1 ships the SCHED source (proc spawn
    / exit), an in-process read visitor, and a versioned binary dump for
    a future offline viewer.  OFF by default and one branch when a
    source is disabled, so it is safe to compile into production.  The
    MSG / IO / OS sources, on-disk spill, and viewer are staged
    follow-ons.  See xtc_tail(3).
  * New XTC_DOWN_KIND_NOCONNECTION down-kind (cross-process channel
    death).  New installed headers xtc_xproc.h, xtc_tail.h.  New DST
    coverage: test_sim_reg gains a dup-key membership + drop_pid
    scenario (deterministic, replay-identical).

 -- Greg Burd <greg@burd.me>  Fri, 10 Jul 2026 18:57:22 -0400

libxtc (1.11.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * OTP-behaviour build-out, part 2 (patterns R5-R10 from the survey),
    completing the R1-R10 program begun in 1.10.0.  All optional,
    opt-in, single-node, and built on the existing proc / registry /
    channel primitives.
  * Registry duplicate-key membership (xtc_reg_register_dup,
    xtc_reg_unregister_pid, xtc_reg_members): many pids may share one
    key, so a key names a group -- the substrate for pub/sub.  (The
    monitor-proc auto-unregister-on-DOWN half remains a tracked
    follow-on.)
  * xtc_pg process groups: xtc_pg_join / xtc_pg_leave / xtc_pg_members /
    xtc_pg_send over the duplicate-key registry -- the Erlang pg /
    Phoenix PubSub / Discord-presence pattern.  Broadcast collects
    members under the registry lock and sends outside it, so no
    bounded-mailbox park happens with the lock held.  See xtc_pg(3).
  * xtc_pool bounded resource pool: xtc_pool_create / _add / _checkout /
    _checkin / _available / _capacity.  Fibers check resources out and
    return them; checkout blocks (with timeout) when all are busy, so
    the pool is also a concurrency limiter -- the checkout/return half
    of the connection-pool pattern.  Built on a counting semaphore.
    See xtc_pool(3).
  * examples/10_circuit_breaker.c: the CLOSED/OPEN/HALF_OPEN circuit
    breaker as an xtc_fsm, demonstrating state_enter, a cooldown state
    timeout, and synchronous call/reply -- and the gen_statem trap that
    a real event cancels a pending state timeout.
  * xtc_chan_demand demand-driven channel: the reactive-streams /
    GenStage backpressure primitive.  A consumer asks for N items; the
    producer's send succeeds only while demand is outstanding.  See
    xtc_chan(3).
  * xtc_stream async streams: a lazy pull-based sequence with map /
    filter / for_each combinators and an xtc_stream_from_demand adapter
    over the demand channel -- the Elixir Stream / Rust Iterator shape.
    See xtc_stream(3).
  * xtc_reg_drop_pid: remove a pid from EVERY key it appears under -- the
    "process left everything" cleanup an embedder calls when a process
    exits or a connection closes (the honest partial answer to the
    not-yet-built automatic monitor-on-DOWN).
  * xtc_credit sliding-window regulator: cap operations kept in flight
    (acquire before issue, release on ack; acquire parks the fiber when
    the window is full), with a peak-in-flight high-water mark.  The
    transport-agnostic request-issue/ack-return sibling of the demand
    channel -- the Erlang :jobs / RabbitMQ credit_flow / GenStage
    max_demand pattern packaged as a primitive.  See xtc_credit(3).
  * Examples put the new blocks to work: rexis gains real Redis Pub/Sub
    (SUBSCRIBE / UNSUBSCRIBE / PUBLISH) on xtc_pg, leaving all channels
    via xtc_reg_drop_pid on disconnect; kaka's credit-window self-test
    is refactored onto xtc_credit.
  * New installed headers xtc_pg.h, xtc_pool.h, xtc_stream.h,
    xtc_credit.h.  No behavior change to existing APIs.

 -- Greg Burd <greg@burd.me>  Fri, 10 Jul 2026 09:28:20 -0400

libxtc (1.10.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * OTP-behaviour build-out, part 1 (from the OTP-patterns survey).  New
    optional, opt-in orchestration behaviours; all shared-nothing-safe
    and buildable on the existing proc/supervisor primitives.
  * New xtc_fsm: a gen_statem-style finite state machine running as a
    process.  Standardizes the three things hand-rolled C switch machines
    get wrong -- state_enter (enter callback on every transition),
    postponed events (XTC_FSM_POSTPONE stashes and replays them oldest-
    first after the next transition), and state timeouts (a synthetic
    timeout event, cancelled by any real event).  Async events via
    xtc_fsm_send, synchronous request/reply via xtc_fsm_call /
    xtc_fsm_reply, lifecycle via start/stop/join/pid.  See xtc_fsm(3).
  * Supervisor: bounded dynamic pool.  xtc_sup_opts_t.max_children caps
    the live child count for the simple_one_for_one strategy;
    xtc_sup_add_child returns XTC_E_RESOURCE at the cap (a slot freed by
    a child DOWN is reusable) -- the connection-pool pattern.  New
    xtc_sup_n_alive (live count vs xtc_sup_n_children total).  The
    one_for_all / rest_for_one / simple_one_for_one strategies were
    already implemented; the header comment is corrected.
  * gen_server handle_continue: a callback arms a continuation with
    xtc_svr_continue(cont), and handle_continue(state, cont) runs before
    the server's next receive -- so init can return fast (unblocking the
    supervisor and the start caller) and finish expensive setup before
    the first message, without a self-send that races an incoming
    message.  See xtc_svr(3).
  * Docs: a fibers-chapter section (and tested snippet) showing the
    stackful-fiber advantage -- suspending from inside a foreign C call
    chain such as qsort()'s comparator, which a callback runtime cannot
    do.
  * No behavior change to existing APIs; xtc_svr_callbacks_t gains a
    handle_continue field before terminate (recompile embedders that use
    a positional initializer).

 -- Greg Burd <greg@burd.me>  Fri, 10 Jul 2026 07:35:39 -0400

libxtc (1.9.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Three open concurrency bugs resolved, all in the cross-thread
    coroutine-lifecycle / signal-mask family:
    - Signal-mask residual: the ucontext coroutine substrate restored a
      fiber's captured (possibly permissive) uc_sigmask onto whatever
      runtime thread ran the fiber, letting a process-directed signal
      (e.g. SIGCHLD) land on a scheduler thread.  The fiber uc_sigmask
      now blocks process-directed signals but exempts SIGVTALRM
      (preemption) and the synchronous fault signals SIGSEGV/SIGBUS/
      SIGFPE/SIGILL/SIGABRT (so R1 fault containment still works); a
      fork'ed child resets its own mask.  test_thread_sigmask is now a
      gating test.
    - xtc_exec_fini cross-thread-spawn coro leak (run-queue case): a
      short-lived cross-thread-spawned coro could reach DONE before the
      spawner stored t->cleanup, causing recycle-as-plain that leaked
      the coro and set up a latent use-after-free.  Fixed by keying the
      recycle path on t->fn != __xtc_coro_step.
    - epoll test_bufmgr_mt lost-wakeup residual: closed by the v1.8.0
      cross-thread prepare/park wake fix (130/130 clean on epoll).
  * New public API: xtc_env_get / xtc_env_set (thread-safe environment
    access), xtc_rand_u64 / xtc_rand_seed (per-thread seedable PRNG),
    xtc_strlcpy / xtc_strlcat (always-NUL-terminating bounded copy) --
    three POSIX/libc sharp-edge smoothers.  See xtc_env(3) and the
    guide's sharp-edges chapter.
  * New release gate [C6]: installed public headers may only define
    XTC_-namespaced macros -- never a standard name (guards the
    ssize_t-macro namespace-pollution class).
  * xtc_bdev.h ssize_t is provided portably without a #define
    (carried from 1.8.0 groundwork).

 -- Greg Burd <greg@burd.me>  Thu, 09 Jul 2026 10:22:17 -0400

libxtc (1.8.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * New public API: xtc_proc_wake(pid) -- resume a process parked in
    xtc_proc_wait_fd or xtc_recv from ANY OS thread, including one libxtc
    does not manage.  The explicit "poke the target loop" primitive an
    embedder calls after a foreign thread makes a watched condition true
    (writes a self-pipe an fd-park watches, sets a latch, completes an
    async read).  Delivers no message; the woken proc re-evaluates, so a
    spurious wake is always safe.  See xtc_proc(3).
  * Fixed a cross-thread wake miss (prepare/park race) in the runtime: a
    cross-thread wake that arrived while the target task was still
    RUNNING -- between arming its waker and the loop transitioning it to
    PARKED on yield -- was dropped by the inbox WAKE drain.  The drain
    now latches a pending-wake when the task is not yet parked, and the
    RUNNING->PARKED transition consumes it and re-schedules instead of
    parking, so a wake in that window is never lost.  Verified on both
    epoll and io_uring.  This is the cross-thread fd/latch wake miss the
    PostgreSQL carrier reported.
  * xtc_bdev.h no longer leaks a bare "#define ssize_t" into consumers:
    an installed public header must never redefine a standard name.  It
    now uses the standard ssize_t directly, provided portably without a
    #define and without redefining it where the platform already has it.
  * CI hardening: a Valgrind job (memcheck gates a curated subset;
    Helgrind and DRD run informationally), coverage tests for svr.c and
    io_common.c edge paths, and tag-triggered release workflows on both
    GitHub and Codeberg that build and attach the source tarball, the
    single-file amalgamation, and the man-page bundle.
  * Windows IOCP: the MSVC smoke test now runtime-verifies multi-op
    native file AIO and cross-thread wakeup coalescing on the reference
    host; the loopback-socket AFD-poll echo remains an open item
    (documented in docs/KNOWN_ISSUES.md).

